This research paper explores the properties of generalized prolate spheroidal wave functions in relation to a given Sturm-Liouville operator. The analysis reveals that these functions, denoted as $\psi_{
u,N}$, can be used to derive the eigenvalues of the operator. The paper demonstrates that the eigenvalue of the operator can be approximated by a ratio of integrals involving $\psi_{
u,N}$ and the function $k(x)$, with the accuracy of this approximation depending on the parameter $N$. The study further establishes that the approximation becomes increasingly accurate as $N$ approaches infinity, with the error decreasing at a rate proportional to $N^{-1}$. The research also investigates the behavior of the function $\psi_{
u,N}$ and its derivative as $x$ approaches infinity, showing that the function and its derivative both decay at a rate proportional to $x^{-1}$. The analysis is supported by numerical examples, which illustrate the accuracy of the approximations and the behavior of the function $\psi_{
u,N}$ for different values of $N$. The research has potential applications in the field of signal processing, where the function $\psi_{
u,N}$ can be used to model the behavior of signals in the presence of noise.
